          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "**Seek urgent help now if you have chest pain, severe breathlessness, fainting, a racing or irregular heartbeat, new confusion, or new weakness on one side of the body.** Also contact a clinician promptly if your breathlessness is getting worse, a fever returns, or you feel clearly worse rather than slowly better. The urgent-symptom list above is general safety advice, not something from the documents searched. The red-flag check found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. Tiredness that is not improving should still be reviewed by a clinician.\n\nOn whether two weeks of tiredness is a concern, the sources do not say how long post-flu fatigue normally lasts, so I cannot tell you whether yours is typical. The retrieved material, which covers other conditions, points to some possibilities a clinician might consider: slow recovery from a respiratory infection, sleep problems that persist after the illness, and lingering cough or breathlessness if you have asthma or COPD. These are things to discuss, not explanations of your symptoms. If you have asthma, COPD, heart disease or diabetes, flu can worsen these, so a review matters more.\n\nOn antivirals, the guidance says they work best when started within 48 hours of symptoms starting. Treatment after that is still described as appropriate for people in hospital or severely ill, and that later-treatment advice rests on observational evidence. Because your flu was two weeks ago, the guidance on starting antivirals is aimed at early treatment. Still, ask a clinician whether any further treatment is relevant to you.\n\nOn vaccination, US guidance routinely recommends an annual vaccine for everyone aged 6 months and older without a contraindication. The usual aim in the northern hemisphere is before the end of October, and later vaccination is still worthwhile while the virus is circulating. I could not retrieve the national guidance on who should be vaccinated, so I cannot summarize that part.\n\nOn evidence, the passages I retrieved do not grade the strength of evidence for vaccination timing or the antiviral recommendations. The only strength statements are a \"modest reduction\" in how long symptoms last with early antivirals, and the observational basis for treatment after 48 hours. The national antiviral guidance has a section on disagreements between authorities, but I did not retrieve it, so I cannot say what those disagreements are.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
